Title:
Decrement by one to rule them all: AsIO3.sys driver exploitation

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
This Talos deep dive analyzes two critical vulnerabilities in ASUS Armoury Crate/AI Suite’s AsIO3.sys kernel driver: a stack-based buffer overflow in Win32-to-NT path conversion (CVE-2025-1533) and an authorization bypass in the IRP_MJ_CREATE access check (CVE-2025-3464). The driver attempts to restrict access by hashing the caller’s image path and comparing it to a hardcoded SHA256 for AsusCertService.exe, but the check can be bypassed using hardlink swapping. With device access, the author identifies multiple dangerous IOCTL primitives and ultimately leverages an IOCTL that calls ObfDereferenceObject on a user-controlled pointer, yielding a “decrement-by-one” arbitrary kernel memory modification (at Object-0x30). The exploit uses a kernel address leak (pre-Win11 24H2) via NtQuerySystemInformation(SystemHandleInformation) to find the current KTHREAD, decrements KTHREAD->PreviousMode from 1 to 0, and then uses unrestricted kernel read/write via syscalls to steal the SYSTEM token by walking EPROCESS ActiveProcessLinks. It also discusses how Windows 11 24H2 restricts kernel module/address leaks via NtQuerySystemInformation, impacting exploit reliability. Useful for kernel exploit developers, red teamers abusing LOLDrivers, and defenders assessing third-party driver risk and access-control design.

Technical Focus:
- Windows kernel driver exploitation (AsIO3.sys) and IRP_MJ_CREATE authorization logic
- Hardlink-based image-path/hash authorization bypass
- IOCTL abuse primitives (ObfDereferenceObject misuse, physical memory mapping, MSR access filtering)
- KTHREAD->PreviousMode manipulation to bypass user/kernel address probing
- Token stealing via EPROCESS traversal (ActiveProcessLinks) and token reference handling
- Impact of Windows 11 24H2 KASLR/leak restrictions (NtQuerySystemInformation hardening)

Use Cases:
- Local privilege escalation to NT AUTHORITY\SYSTEM via vulnerable third-party drivers
- Auditing/triaging OEM “hardware utility” drivers for dangerous IOCTL exposure
- Developing mitigations/detections for LOLDriver-style abuse (device ACLs, IOCTL monitoring)
- Researching exploitation patterns around PreviousMode flips and handle-table leaks

Keywords:
AsIO3.sys, ASUS Armoury Crate, AI Suite, LOLDriver, CVE-2025-1533, CVE-2025-3464, TALOS-2025-2144, TALOS-2025-2150, IRP_MJ_CREATE, IOCTL, ObfDereferenceObject, reference count decrement, KTHREAD, PreviousMode, EPROCESS, ActiveProcessLinks, token stealing, ZwQueryInformationProcess, ProcessImageFileNameWin32, NtQuerySystemInformation, SystemHandleInformation, Windows 11 24H2, kernel address leak, SHA256, hardlink bypass, MSR, IA32_LSTAR, physical memory mapping